<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#elamite-language">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elamite language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Elamite administrative language</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elamite is the extinct language of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, written first in its own scripts and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>. It has no known relatives. It served as an administrative language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation</a>, and its continued use by the early Achaemenid administration is one of Elam's lasting legacies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elamite-language.jpg" alt="Elamite language" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ramessos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ElamischeStrichschrift.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Elamite, also called Hatamtite and formerly Scythic, Median, Amardian, Anshanian and Susian, was spoken by the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> and is recorded in what is now south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from about 2600 BCE to 330 BCE. It is generally thought to have no demonstrable relatives, and the lack of relatives makes its interpretation difficult. Most linguists regard it as a language isolate, unrelated to Semitic, Indo-European or Sumerian, despite its adoption of the Sumerian-Akkadian cuneiform script. An Elamo-Dravidian family linking it to Brahui and the Dravidian languages was proposed in 1967 by Igor M. Diakonoff, but a reassessment published in 2023 reaffirmed that it lacks good evidence, and none of the proposed relationships has been accepted by mainstream historical linguists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Elamite is divided into periods: Old Elamite (c. 2600 to 1500 BCE), Middle Elamite (c. 1500 to 1000 BCE), regarded as the classical period, Neo-Elamite (1000 to 550 BCE) and Achaemenid Elamite (550 to 330 BCE), the best-attested variety. It was written first in Proto-Elamite, the oldest known writing system from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> (c. 3100 to 2900 BCE), which is not deciphered, and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-elamite">Linear Elamite</a>, used for a brief time in the last quarter of the third millennium BCE and at least partly deciphered. Elamite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, adapted from Akkadian, was largely a syllabary of some 130 signs; its corpus is about 20,000 tablets and fragments, most from the Achaemenid era and mainly economic records.</p><p>It is an agglutinative language, mostly suffixing, with a noun class system that combines animate and inanimate nouns with personal classes matching the three persons of verbal inflection. Word order is subject, object, verb. A king might be written sunki-k, meaning I, a king, and sunki-r, a king in the third person.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The Persepolis Administrative Archives, found in the 1930s, are mostly in Elamite; the remains of more than 10,000 documents record administrative activity at Persepolis over more than fifty years, from 509 to 457 BCE, against only about 1,000 original records in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>. Elamite was also used for official royal inscriptions, including the trilingual inscriptions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. The study of Elamite began with the publication of Achaemenid royal inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the early 19th century. Edwin Norris published the Elamite version of the Bisutun Inscription in 1855, and Jules Oppert published the first grammar in 1879. Archibald Henry Sayce is considered the first to use the name Elamite, in 1874.</p><p>The language may have remained in use after the Achaemenid period: several rulers of Elymais bore the Elamite name Kamnaskires in the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE. Arabic authors of the 8th to 13th centuries refer to a language called Khuzi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzistan</a>, possibly a late variant of Elamite, which probably went extinct in the 11th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elamite language” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most significant of all, <b>Elamite language</b> and bureaucratic practice were absorbed into the Achaemenid Persian state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
